Java Records vs. Value Types (Project Valhalla)

Historically, Java forced a compromise: Primitives offered high performance but lacked object-oriented programming (OOP) capabilities. Objects offered full identity and polymorphism but carried massive pointer overhead and poor cache locality.

1. Java Records (Structural Cleanliness, Not Performance Fixes)

Introduced as stable features in Java 16, Records are data-carrier classes.
• What they fix: They eliminate boilerplate code by automatically generating fields, getters, equals(), hashCode(), and toString().
• The Reality: At runtime, Records are still heavy identity objects allocated on the heap. They do not solve the heap allocation performance gap.

2. Project Valhalla Value Types (The True Performance Bridge)

Project Valhalla resolves this design tradeoff by introducing Value Objects (using the phrase "Codes like a class, works like an int").
Valhalla splits objects into two distinct runtime classifications:
1. Identity Objects: Standard Java objects (can be mutated, synchronized on, and checked with == memory comparison).
2. Value Objects: Objects that lack identity. They are completely immutable aggregates of fields. Because they lack an individual identity address, the JVM can completely optimize them.

The Architectural Shift

Traditional Heap Object (Pointer Chasing):
[Stack Reference] ──> [Heap Object Header (8-16 bytes)] ──> [Actual Data Values]

Valhalla Value Objects (Flat Memory Layout):
[Stack/Array Allocation] ──> [Actual Data Values Stored Inline directly next to each other]
• Flattened Data Layout: Instead of an array of pointers pointing to scattered heap locations, an array of Value Objects is laid out flat and sequentially in memory—exactly like a primitive array.
• Zero Object Header Overhead: Because they have no identity, the runtime strips away the standard 8-to-16-byte object header metadata.
• CPU Cache Optimizations: Flat memory allocation maximizes L1/L2 CPU cache lines. The CPU can fetch sequential values simultaneously without forcing the thread to stall while waiting to resolve multiple heap pointers.


