Using Built-in Functional Interfaces (Recommended)

Java provides a standard package (java.util.function) containing built-in interfaces that cover almost every common method signature:
• Function<T, R>: Takes an argument of type T, returns a result of type R.
• Consumer<T>: Takes an argument of type T, returns nothing (void).
• Supplier<T>: Takes no arguments, returns a result of type T.
• Runnable: Takes no arguments, returns nothing (void).
Here is a complete example passing a method that transforms a string:
java
import java.util.function.Function;

public class MethodParamExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Pass a method reference using the :: operator
        executeAction("hello world", MethodParamExample::convertToUpperCase);

        // Alternatively, you can pass a lambda expression directly
        executeAction("java", str -> str + " language");
    }

    // This method accepts another method as a parameter
    public static void executeAction(String target, Function<String, String> algorithm) {
        // Execute the passed method using .apply()
        String result = algorithm.apply(target); 
        System.out.println("Result: " + result);
    }

    // The method we want to pass
    public static String convertToUpperCase(String input) {
        return input.toUpperCase();
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Using Custom Functional Interfaces

If your method requires a custom layout (for example, passing 3 or 4 parameters), you can create your own functional interface using the @FunctionalInterface annotation. A functional interface is simply an interface with exactly one abstract method.
java
public class CustomParamExample {

    // 1. Define the functional interface
    @FunctionalInterface
    interface MathOperation {
        int compute(int a, int b, int c); // Target signature
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 3. Pass the method reference matching the interface signature
        int result = processMath(5, 10, 2, CustomParamExample::addAndMultiply);
        System.out.println("Math Result: " + result); // Output: 30
    }

    // 2. Create a method that accepts the interface as a parameter
    public static int processMath(int x, int y, int z, MathOperation operation) {
        return operation.compute(x, y, z);
    }

    // The method matching the abstract method layout
    public static int addAndMultiply(int a, int b, int c) {
        return (a + b) * c;
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Quick Reference Cheat Sheet


If your method looks like:	Use this Java Interface:	How to invoke it inside your receiver:
void myMethod()	Runnable	interfaceParam.run();
void myMethod(String s)	Consumer<String>	interfaceParam.accept(s);
String myMethod()	Supplier<String>	String s = interfaceParam.get();
Integer myMethod(String s)	Function<String, Integer>	Integer i = interfaceParam.apply(s);
boolean myMethod(int price)	Predicate<Integer>	boolean b = interfaceParam.test(price);


