 The Basic Record Example

Instead of writing 50+ lines of standard POJO boilerplate code, you can define a record using the record keyword.
java
// That's it! This single line creates an immutable data class.
public record Employee(int id, String name, double salary) {}
Use code with caution.

What the Compiler Automatically Generates Behind the Scenes:

• private final fields for id, name, and salary.
• A Canonical Constructor that accepts all arguments to initialize the fields.
• Public accessor methods (getters) named exactly like the fields (id(), name(), salary()) rather than getId().
• toString(), equals(), and hashCode() implementations based on the record components.

2. How to Use the Record

You instantiate and use a record exactly like a regular class, but your data is immutable (read-only):
java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Create an instance (Calls the auto-generated canonical constructor)
        Employee emp = new Employee(101, "Alice Smith", 85000.0);

        // 2. Access data using the auto-generated getter methods
        System.out.println("Employee Name: " + emp.name()); // Output: Alice Smith
        System.out.println("Employee Salary: " + emp.salary()); // Output: 85000.0

        // 3. Print the object (Calls the auto-generated toString method)
        System.out.println(emp); 
        // Output: Employee[id=101, name=Alice Smith, salary=85000.0]

        // 4. Check for equality (Calls the auto-generated equals method)
        Employee emp2 = new Employee(101, "Alice Smith", 85000.0);
        System.out.println(emp.equals(emp2)); // Output: true (Compares values, not references)
    }
}
Use code with caution.

3. Adding Validations: The Compact Constructor

If you need to validate data before it gets assigned to the fields, you can use a Compact Constructor. This constructor does not require parameters or manual field assignment (this.x = x) because Java handles that automatically right after validation.
java
public record Employee(int id, String name, double salary) {
    
    // Compact constructor for validation
    public Employee {
        if (id <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("ID must be greater than zero.");
        }
        if (salary < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Salary cannot be negative.");
        }
        if (name == null || name.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Name cannot be null or empty.");
        }
        // No need to write: this.id = id; (Java does it implicitly!)
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Key Rules to Remember 💡

1. Immutability: Fields are final by default. There are no setter methods generated, and you cannot alter data once the object is created.
2. Inheritance: Records implicitly extend java.lang.Record and are marked final. This means a record cannot extend another class, nor can any class extend a record.
3. Interfaces: While they cannot use class inheritance, records can implement interfaces.
