 How Properties and YML Files Are Chosen (The Priority Hierarchy)

If you have both an application.properties and an application.yml file in the same folder, application.properties takes precedence and will override any conflicting keys in the YML file. However, it is a best practice to use only one format in your project to prevent confusion.
Spring Boot uses a highly strict Externalized Configuration Order to determine which values win. If a key is defined in multiple locations, the location highest on this list wins and overwrites the others:
1. Command-Line Arguments: (e.g., java -jar app.jar --server.port=9090) -> Highest priority.
2. OS Environment Variables: (e.g., setting SERVER_PORT=9090 in your system environment).
3. Profile-Specific Application Properties (Outside the jar): application-{profile}.properties or .yml placed in the folder where the jar is running.
4. Profile-Specific Application Properties (Inside the jar): application-{profile}.properties or .yml packaged inside your classpath.
5. Standard Application Properties (Outside the jar): Packaged in an external configuration directory.
6. Standard Application Properties (Inside the jar): The default src/main/resources/application.properties or .yml -> Lowest priority.

