In Spring Boot, there are three primary strategies to define and create beans. They range from fully automated scanning to manual programmatic definition.
Choosing the right strategy depends on whether you own the source code of the class or need to configure third-party libraries.

1. The Automated Strategy (Stereotype Annotations)

This is the most common approach. You mark your classes with Stereotype Annotations, and Spring's Component Scanner automatically discovers, instantiates, and manages them as beans during startup.
• When to use: For standard application classes that you write and own (e.g., your business logic, controllers, repositories).
• Key Annotations:
	• @Component: The generic baseline marker for any Spring-managed bean.
	• @Service: Denotes facade classes containing business logic.
	• @Repository: Used for data access layers (DAOs/Repositories); it automatically translates database exceptions into Spring's data exception hierarchy.
	• @RestController / @Controller: Declares web-facing controllers handling HTTP traffic.

Code Example:

java
package com.example.demo.service;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service // Spring automatically finds this and turns it into a bean
public class PalindromeService {
    public boolean check(String text) {
        return new StringBuilder(text).reverse().toString().equals(text);
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. The Programmatic Strategy (Configuration Classes)

Instead of letting Spring guess, you take control by writing a configuration class annotated with @Configuration. Inside, you write methods annotated with @Bean. The return value of that method is registered as a bean in the container.
• When to use:
	• When configuring third-party libraries (e.g., Jackson ObjectMappers, data sources, security filters) where you cannot open the source code to add @Component.
	• When bean creation logic requires complex setup conditional statements.
• Key Annotations: @Configuration (class level) and @Bean (method level).

Code Example:

java
package com.example.demo.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

@Configuration // Tells Spring this class contains manual bean blueprints
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean // The returned ObjectMapper object becomes a Spring Bean
    public ObjectMapper customObjectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        // Configure the third-party object manually
        mapper.findAndRegisterModules();
        return mapper;
    }
}
Use code with caution.

3. The Declarative Strategy (XML Configuration)

This is the legacy way of managing Spring applications. Beans are declared inside an external XML file using <bean> tags.
• When to use: Almost never in modern Spring Boot applications. It is strictly kept for backward compatibility with 10+ year-old enterprise legacy systems.

Code Example (beans.xml):

xml
<beans xmlns="http://springframework.org" ...>
    <bean id="myService" class="com.example.demo.LegacyService" />
</beans>
Summary Strategy Selector


Strategy	Ownership of Code	Setup Style	Configuration Type
Automated (@Component)	🟢 Yes (Your own code)	Implicit / Auto-scanned	Annotation-driven [1]
Programmatic (@Bean)	🔴 No (Third-party libraries)	Explicit / Manual Java code	Java-based configuration
Declarative (<bean>)	🟡 Either (Legacy migration)	Explicit / External file	XML-driven


