What is a HashMap?

A HashMap is a key-value data structure in Java (part of the java.util package) that implements the Map interface. It stores data as Key-Pair associations (e.g., matching an Employee ID to their profile).
• It allows one null key and multiple null values.
• It does not guarantee order; the arrangement of items can change over time as new elements are added.
• It offers near-instant operations, providing O(1) constant time complexity for basic operations like put() and get().

2. The Core Architecture Components

Before looking at the execution flow, you need to understand the underlying infrastructure that lives inside a HashMap:
• The Node Array (Bucket Table): Internally, a HashMap contains an array of nodes (Node<K,V>[] table). Each slot in this array is called a bucket.
• The Node Structure: Each index in the array contains a linked list or tree node containing four fields:
	1. int hash (The calculated hash code)
	2. K key (The actual key object)
	3. V value (The stored data value)
	4. Node<K,V> next (A pointer to the next node in case of collisions)

3. How it Works Internally (Step-by-Step)


A. The Storage Process: map.put(key, value)

When you insert a key-value pair, the HashMap executes a distinct sequence:
[Calculate hashCode()] ➔ [Apply Hash Function] ➔ [Determine Bucket Index] ➔ [Check for Collision] ➔ [Insert Node]
1. Hashing: The HashMap calls the key object's native hashCode() method. It then processes this value through its own internal hash function (shifting bits) to distribute values evenly and prevent clustering.
2. Index Calculation: It converts the final hash into an array boundary index using a bitwise formula:
\(\text{Index}=\text{hash}\ \&\ (n-1)\)
(where n is the current length of the bucket array).
3. The Perfect Fit: If the calculated bucket index is empty, the Node object is placed directly into that array slot.

B. Handling Hash Collisions (The Collision Strategy)

A Hash Collision happens when two completely different keys produce the exact same bucket index calculation.
Java handles collisions using Chaining:
• Linked List Chain: If a bucket is already occupied by a node, the new node is attached to the end of the existing node as a linked list (node.next = newNode).
• Treeification (Java 8 Optimization): If a linked list chain inside a single bucket grows past a threshold of 8 nodes (TREEIFY_THRESHOLD), and the total map capacity is at least 64, Java automatically converts that specific bucket from a slow linked list into a highly optimized Balanced Red-Black Tree. This ensures that even during extreme collisions, worst-case lookup performance drops to \(O(\log N)\) instead of O(N).

C. The Retrieval Process: map.get(key)

When you fetch a value using a key, it mirrors the storage process:
1. It hashes your input key to find the exact target bucket index immediately (O(1)).
2. If multiple elements exist in that bucket (due to a collision), it iterates through the chain.
3. It checks each node using the equals() method (node.key.equals(key)). Once it finds a true match, it extracts and returns the value.

4. Resizing and Capacity Factors

A HashMap cannot grow infinitely without degrading performance. It relies on two properties to determine when to expand:
• Initial Capacity: The baseline size of the internal bucket array when created. The default configuration is 16.
• Load Factor: A metric determining how full the map can get before expanding. The default is 0.75 (75% full).
• The Threshold: Calculated as Capacity × Load Factor. For a default map (16 × 0.75 = 12), the moment you add the 13th element, the HashMap triggers a Resize.
• Rehashing: During a resize, the map doubles its bucket array size (e.g., from 16 to 32). It then loops through every single existing item, recalculates its index using the new array boundaries, and moves it to its new bucket location.



While you cannot completely eliminate hash collisions due to the Pigeonhole Principle (fitting infinite possible object variations into a finite set of integer values), you can drastically minimize their occurrence.
Here are the four engineering strategies used to avoid and mitigate hash collisions in Java.

1. Design a High-Entropy hashCode() Function

A great hash function must distribute keys uniformly across the entire range of integer values. If your hash codes are clustered together, your elements will pile up into the same buckets.

Do Not Write Lazy Hash Functions:

java
// ❌ CRITICAL COLLISION HAZARD: Every single object ends up in the exact same bucket!
@Override
public int hashCode() {
    return 42; 
}
Use code with caution.

Best Practice (Use Prime Multipliers):

Multiply fields by prime numbers (typically 31). Prime numbers reduce structural clustering when combining different field types (like mixing an int with a String).
java
//  RECOMMENDED: Uniformly spreads bit distribution
@Override
public int hashCode() {
    int result = 17;
    result = 31 * result + id;
    result = 31 * result + (name != null ? name.hashCode() : 0);
    return result;
}
Use code with caution.

2. Leverage String and Java Record Defaults

Unless you have highly specialized cryptographic or bitwise performance requirements, do not write custom hash math calculations from scratch.
• Java Strings have a highly optimized, battle-tested hashCode() implementation that minimizes collisions natively.
• Java Records automatically compile a highly optimal hashCode() calculation matching all component fields perfectly.
• java.util.Objects.hash(...) uses a stable variant of the prime-multiplier algorithm under the hood.

3. Enforce Strict Immutability on Map Keys

If a key object's internal fields change after it has been stored inside a HashMap, its hashCode() changes with it. This causes a devastating issue:
1. You call map.put(key, value). The map hashes the key and places it in Bucket 4.
2. You modify a field on the key object (e.g., key.setName("Bob")).
3. You call map.get(key). The map computes the new hash, which points to Bucket 9.
4. The map looks in Bucket 9, finds nothing, and returns null.
The original object is now permanently stuck in Bucket 4, resulting in a silent memory leak and simulated structural collisions. Always mark key fields as private final to guarantee stability.

4. Optimize Capacity Adjustments Upfront

If you know you need to store roughly 500 items, do not initialize a default empty HashMap.
A default map starts with only 16 buckets. As you add items, it continually compresses large hashes down to fit those 16 slots, forcing multiple elements into the same buckets. Even though it automatically doubles in size at the threshold, sizing the map properly upfront keeps the bucket pool large and collisions near zero.
