Spring AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) is a core framework module that allows you to complement traditional Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) by isolating cross-cutting concerns.
In standard applications, common tasks like logging, transaction management, auditing, and security validation are repeated across multiple layers. Instead of duplicating this logic in every service method, Spring AOP allows you to modularize it into standalone components called Aspects and inject them dynamically where needed without altering the underlying business code.

1. Core Architectural Concepts

To master Spring AOP, it is essential to understand its foundational terminology:
• Aspect: A modular unit of a cross-cutting concern. A normal Java class annotated with @Aspect.
• Join Point: A specific point during execution of the program where code execution can be intercepted. In Spring AOP, a Join Point always represents a method execution.
• Advice: The actual action/code executed by an aspect at a particular Join Point (e.g., executing a log statement before or after a method runs).
• Pointcut: An expression that matches specific Join Points to determine where an Advice should run. It defines the target package, class, or method signature.
• Target Object: The application object whose methods are intercepted by the aspect.
• AOP Proxy: A dynamic proxy object created by the Spring framework to implement the aspect contracts (wrapping the target object).

2. Types of Advice

Spring AOP provides five distinct types of advice based on when you want your cross-cutting code to execute relative to the target method:

Advice Type	Annotation	Description
Before	@Before	Runs before the target method executes. Cannot stop execution unless it throws an exception.
After Returning	@AfterReturning	Runs after the target method completes successfully without throwing exceptions.
After Throwing	@AfterThrowing	Runs after the target method finishes by throwing an exception.
After (Finally)	@After	Runs after the target method completes, regardless of its outcome (success or failure).
Around	@Around	The most powerful advice. Surrounds the method invocation, allowing you to control whether, when, and how the target method executes.


3. Step-by-Step Implementation


Step 3.1: Add the AOP Dependency

Add the Spring AOP starter package to your project build file.
Maven (pom.xml):
