Use code with caution.

Step 3.2: Create the Target Business Service

Here is a normal Spring service class that handles payment processing. It should remain clean and completely unaware of logging or tracking concerns.
java
package com.example.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    public String processPayment(String accountId, double amount) {
        System.out.println("Executing core business logic: Processing payment for " + accountId);
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be greater than zero");
        }
        return "SUCCESS_TXN_9921";
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Step 3.3: Implement the Aspect and Pointcuts

Now, build the aspect class to intercept processPayment using different advices.
java
package com.example.aspect;

import org.aspectj.lang.JoinPoint;
import org.aspectj.lang.ProceedingJoinPoint;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.Arrays;

@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {

    // Pointcut expression matching any method in PaymentService
    @Pointcut("execution(* com.example.service.PaymentService.*(..))")
    public void paymentServiceMethods() {}

    // 1. Before Advice: Log method input arguments before it starts
    @Before("paymentServiceMethods()")
    public void logBeforeMethod(JoinPoint joinPoint) {
        String methodName = joinPoint.getSignature().getName();
        Object[] args = joinPoint.getArgs();
        System.out.println(">> [AOP BEFORE] Entering method: " + methodName + " with args: " + Arrays.toString(args));
    }

    // 2. After Returning Advice: Capture the returned output value
    @AfterReturning(pointcut = "paymentServiceMethods()", returning = "result")
    public void logAfterReturning(JoinPoint joinPoint, Object result) {
        System.out.println("<< [AOP AFTER-RETURNING] Method executed successfully. Returned: " + result);
    }

    // 3. After Throwing Advice: Capture exceptions raised by target methods
    @AfterThrowing(pointcut = "paymentServiceMethods()", throwing = "exception")
    public void logAfterThrowing(JoinPoint joinPoint, Throwable exception) {
        System.err.println("!! [AOP AFTER-THROWING] Method encountered an error: " + exception.getMessage());
    }

    // 4. Around Advice: Measure execution performance time
    @Around("paymentServiceMethods()")
    public Object profileExecutionTime(ProceedingJoinPoint proceedingJoinPoint) throws Throwable {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        
        try {
            // Explicitly proceed with target method execution
            Object result = proceedingJoinPoint.proceed(); 
            
            long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            System.out.println("== [AOP AROUND] Execution completed in " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
            return result;
        } catch (Throwable throwable) {
            long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            System.out.println("== [AOP AROUND] Failed execution tracked in " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
            throw throwable; // Rethrow to ensure AfterThrowing advice still catches it
        }
    }
}
Use code with caution.

4. Anatomy of Pointcut Expressions

Pointcut expressions dictate matching rules. The expression format used above is:
execution(modifiers-pattern? ret-type-pattern declaring-type-pattern? name-pattern(param-pattern) throws-pattern?)

Common Examples:

• execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..)): Matches any method inside any class inside the com.example.service package.
• execution(public * *(..)): Matches any public method in the entire application.
• @annotation(com.example.annotation.LogExecution): Matches any method decorated with a custom annotation named @LogExecution.

5. How Spring AOP Works Under the Hood

Spring AOP relies heavily on Runtime Dynamic Proxies. When the application starts up:
1. Spring analyzes classes to see if they match any defined Pointcut rules.
2. If a match is found, Spring does not inject the raw class bean into the ApplicationContext. Instead, it generates a Proxy Object.
3. When another component calls paymentService.processPayment(), it invokes the method on the Proxy.
4. The Proxy executes relevant @Before advice, invokes the actual code via proceed(), and then fires the remaining @After advices.
Note on Internal Calls: Because Spring AOP relies on proxy wrappers, if methodA() inside PaymentService directly calls methodB() within the same class, the proxy is bypassed. Consequently, any aspect attached to methodB() will not trigger.

