Java Optional Class Example

The Optional<T> class (introduced in Java 8) is a container object used to represent the presence or absence of a non-null value. It helps developers avoid explicit null checks and prevent NullPointerException (NPE).

Code Example

Here is a complete example demonstrating how to create, check, and safely extract values using Optional:
java
import java.util.Optional;

public class OptionalExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Creating Optionals
        Optional<String> emptyOptional = Optional.empty();
        Optional<String> valuedOptional = Optional.of("Spring Boot");
        Optional<String> nullableOptional = Optional.ofNullable(null); // Safe even if null

        // 2. Safe Data Extraction with Fallbacks
        
        // returns "Default Value" because nullableOptional contains null
        String fallbackValue = nullableOptional.orElse("Default Value"); 
        System.out.println("orElse Result: " + fallbackValue);

        // returns "SPRING BOOT" because valuedOptional has data
        String transformedValue = valuedOptional.map(String::toUpperCase)
                                                .orElse("UNKNOWN");
        System.out.println("Mapped Result: " + transformedValue);

        // 3. Conditional Execution using ifPresent
        valuedOptional.ifPresent(val -> System.out.println("Value is present: " + val));

        // 4. Throwing custom exceptions if data is missing
        try {
            emptyOptional.orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("Data is missing!"));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Caught Expected Exception: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Core Features of var (Local Variable Type Inference)

Introduced in Java 10, the var keyword allows the compiler to infer the data type of a local variable based on its initialization value.
• Compile-Time Only: It does not introduce dynamic typing. The variable remains strictly typed under the hood; the compiler simply writes the type for you at compilation.
• Local Scope Restriction: It can only be used for local variables inside methods, loops, or static blocks. It cannot be used for class fields, method parameters, or return types.
• Immediate Initialization Mandatory: You cannot declare a var without initializing it on the same line (e.g., var explicit; is invalid).
• Enhances Readability with Generics: It eliminates boilerplate code, especially when working with deeply nested collections:java
// Traditional approach
Map<String, List<User>> userMapping = new HashMap<String, List<User>>();

// Using var
var userMapping = new HashMap<String, List<User>>();
Use code with caution.
• Cannot handle null/lambda inferences directly: You cannot initialize a var to null directly (var x = null; fails) or assign a standalone lambda expression without an explicit cast.

3. Checked Exceptions: Advantages and Disadvantages

Checked exceptions are exceptions that are checked by the compiler at compile-time. If a method throws a checked exception (e.g., IOException, SQLException), the calling code must either handle it using a try-catch block or declare it in its method signature using the throws keyword.

Advantages

• Enforced Reliability: They force developers to anticipate and explicitly handle common environmental runtime failures (like missing files or network dropouts).
• Self-Documenting Code: The method signature explicitly states what can go wrong, acting as a clear contract for anyone consuming your API.

Disadvantages

• Code Bloat & Boilerplate: Developers often end up writing extensive try-catch blocks that clutter the primary business logic.
• Erosion of Architecture Layers: Lower-level exceptions (like SQLException) frequently ripple upwards through service interfaces to controller layers, forcing higher layers to adapt to infrastructure concerns.
• Swallowing Exceptions: Frustrated developers often catch exceptions and leave the block empty (catch(Exception e) {}), which silently swallows critical bugs.

4. The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) & Inheritance


Explaining LSP

The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) states that objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of its subclasses without breaking the application. In simpler terms, a subclass must honor the behavioral contract established by its parent class. It must not alter the expected behavior, weaken pre-conditions, or strengthen post-conditions.
• Classic violation example: A Square extending a Rectangle. A rectangle allows you to change width and height independently. If setting a Square's width implicitly modifies its height, it violates the parent class's behavior and breaks LSP.

If a subclass can replace its parent, why is inheritance required?

The phrasing of this question highlights a common conceptual knot: LSP is a constraint on inheritance, not an alternative to it.
Inheritance is required to establish the foundational relationship that allows substitution to happen in the first place. Here is why we use them together:
• Polymorphism & Abstraction: Inheritance allows you to write decoupled code that targets a broad, abstract parent type. Without inheritance, you could not pass diverse implementations into a single component.
• LSP is the Rulebook for Inheritance: Inheritance provides the mechanism for code reuse and subtyping, while LSP provides the safety rules. LSP ensures that when you leverage inheritance, you do not accidentally create fragile, broken hierarchies that surprise the application at runtime.
• Code Sharing: Inheritance prevents you from writing identical logic across dozens of standalone classes by allowing common state and behavior to live in a single superclass.


 Identical Default Methods in Multiple Interfaces

When a class implements two interfaces containing identical default methods (same name and parameter signatures), Java throws a compile-time error due to the Diamond Problem. The compiler cannot implicitly guess which interface implementation should take precedence.

Resolution Strategy

The implementing class must override the conflicting method and manually specify which default behavior to use, or provide an entirely new implementation. You can explicitly call a specific parent interface's method using the syntax InterfaceName.super.methodName().
java
interface InterfaceA {
    default void logMessage() { System.out.println("Message from A"); }
}

interface InterfaceB {
    default void logMessage() { System.out.println("Message from B"); }
}

// Resolving the conflict
class LoggerImplementation implements InterfaceA, InterfaceB {
    @Override
    public void logMessage() {
        // Manually resolving ambiguity by picking InterfaceA's implementation
        InterfaceA.super.logMessage(); 
        
        // Alternatively, you can add custom logic here
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Types of Methods Defined in a Java Interface

Modern Java permits four types of methods inside an interface:
• Abstract Methods: The traditional default type. They have no body and must be implemented by concrete subclasses.
• Default Methods: Introduced in Java 8. Methods containing a body that provide a fallback implementation so implementing classes do not break when new methods are added.
• Static Methods: Introduced in Java 8. Belong to the interface class itself and cannot be overridden by implementing classes. They are called via InterfaceName.staticMethod().
• Private Methods (and Private Static): Introduced in Java 9. Used to encapsulate and share redundant code blocks between multiple default or static methods within the same interface.

3. Interface vs. Abstract Class


Criterion	Interface	Abstract Class
Inheritance Model	A class can implement multiple interfaces.	A class can extend only one abstract class.
State / Fields	Cannot hold instance state. All variables are implicitly public static final.	Can hold instance fields (state) with any access modifier (private, protected).
Constructors	Cannot have constructors.	Can have constructors to initialize subclass state.
Primary Intent	Defines a peripheral capability or behavioral contract ("can-do" relationship).	Defines a core identity and shared structural base ("is-a" relationship).


4. Factory Design Pattern

The Factory Pattern abstracts the instantiation logic of objects, allowing a client to create instances without coupling itself to concrete implementation classes.

Modified Implementation (Using Java 8+ Functional Interfaces and Map Registry)

Instead of a rigid switch-case statement that violates the Open/Closed Principle, this modified version uses a dynamic registry map.
java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

// 1. Common Product Interface
interface Notification {
    void send(String message);
}

// 2. Concrete Implementations
class SMSNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(String msg) { System.out.println("SMS sent: " + msg); }
}
class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(String msg) { System.out.println("Email sent: " + msg); }
}

// 3. Modified Factory using a Functional Registry
class NotificationFactory {
    private static final Map<String, Supplier<Notification>> registry = new HashMap<>();

    // Register products dynamically without editing the factory class body later
    static {
        registry.put("SMS", SMSNotification::new);
        registry.put("EMAIL", EmailNotification::new);
    }

    public static Notification createNotification(String type) {
        Supplier<Notification> supplier = registry.get(type.toUpperCase());
        if (supplier == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown notification type: " + type);
        }
        return supplier.get();
    }
}
Use code with caution.

5. The volatile Keyword

The volatile keyword is a visibility modifier used in multi-threaded programming. It guarantees that changes made to a variable by one thread are immediately visible to all other threads.

How it Works:

• Bypasses CPU Cache: Normally, threads cache shared variables in CPU registers for performance. volatile forces the application to read and write the variable directly from the Main Memory (RAM).
• Prevents Instruction Reordering: It establishes a memory barrier, ensuring the compiler does not reorder statements around it for optimization purposes.
• Crucial Limitation: It does not provide mutual exclusion (locking) and does not make compound operations atomic.

6. volatile vs. Atomic Classes


Feature	volatile	Atomic Classes (AtomicInteger, etc.)
Thread Safety Level	Solves Visibility issues only.	Solves both Visibility and Atomicity issues.
Compound Operations	Unsafe for operations like read-modify-write (count++).	Safe for compound actions via hardware-level operations.
Underlying Mechanism	Direct Main Memory access / CPU cache flushing.	Compare-And-Swap (CAS) non-blocking CPU instructions.


7. Key CompletableFuture Methods

CompletableFuture is used for asynchronous, non-blocking programming in Java.
• supplyAsync(Supplier<U>): Runs an asynchronous task on a background thread pool (ForkJoinPool) and returns a result.
• thenApply(Function<T,R>): A synchronous callback that transforms the result of the previous stage once it finishes (similar to map).
• thenCompose(Function<T,CompletableFuture<U>>): Flattens nested futures. Used when the transformation step itself returns another CompletableFuture (similar to flatMap).
• thenCombine(CompletionStage, BiFunction): Runs two independent asynchronous futures concurrently and combines their results when both complete.
• exceptionally(Function<Throwable, T>): Acts as a catch block to handle errors gracefully by returning a fallback value.

8. Difference Between map and flatMap in Streams

Both methods transform stream data, but they handle the structure of the output differently:
• map(): Transforms each element into one single value. If the mapping function returns a collection, you end up with a nested stream (e.g., Stream<List<String>>).
• flatMap(): Transforms each element into a Stream of values and then merges all those individual streams into a single, continuous, unified stream (e.g., flattens Stream<List<String>> into Stream<String>).

9. Internal Working of the Spring Container

The Spring Container (ApplicationContext) manages the lifecycle of beans using the following internal flow:
[Read Definitions] ➔ [BeanFactoryPostProcessor] ➔ [Instantiate Beans] ➔ [Populate Properties] ➔ [BeanPostProcessor] ➔ [Ready]
1. Resource Loading: The container reads configuration metadata (Java Config annotations like @Configuration, @Component, or XML files).
2. Bean Definition Creation: Metadata is parsed into internal BeanDefinition objects, which serve as blueprints.
3. BeanFactoryPostProcessing: Custom interceptors (BeanFactoryPostProcessor) modify the metadata blueprints before any objects are instantiated (e.g., replacing ${db.url} placeholders with real property values).
4. Instantiation & Dependency Injection: The container physically creates instances using reflection and resolves properties via @Autowired.
5. Initialization Interception: The container applies BeanPostProcessor engines to wrap the fresh beans (e.g., wrapping them in AOP proxies for transaction management).

10. The Spring Bean Lifecycle

The lifecycle of a single Spring-managed bean consists of four core phases:
[1. Instantiation] ➔ [2. Populate Properties] ➔ [3. Initialization Stage] ➔ [4. Destruction Stage]

Detailed Stages:

1. Instantiation: The constructor executes to allocate object memory.
2. Populate Properties: Setter methods and field injections execute (@Autowired).
3. Aware Interfaces: If the bean implements interfaces like BeanNameAware or ApplicationContextAware, Spring injects the container context into it.
4. Pre-Initialization: BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization() runs.
5. Initialization: The custom init methods execute in this exact sequence:
	• Methods annotated with @PostConstruct.
	• afterPropertiesSet() from the InitializingBean interface.
	• Any custom initMethod defined on @Bean.
6. Post-Initialization: BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization() runs (where AOP proxies are generated). The bean is now Ready for Use.
7. Destruction: When the context shuts down, destruction cleanups run in this sequence:
	• Methods annotated with @PreDestroy.
	• destroy() from the DisposableBean interface.
	• Any custom destroyMethod declaration.

11. Fetch Types: Lazy vs. Eager Loading

Fetch types dictate how Hibernate / JPA fetches associated child relationships from the database when loading a parent entity.

Eager Loading (FetchType.EAGER)

• Behavior: The parent and all associated child collections/entities are loaded immediately in a single database operation (typically using an SQL OUTER JOIN).
• When to Use: When you know the application will absolutely use the child data every single time it accesses the parent.
• Risk: Can trigger severe performance degradation if applied to large datasets, leading to massive memory overhead.

Lazy Loading (FetchType.LAZY)

• Behavior: The database fetches only the parent record first. Associated child data is replaced with a lightweight "Proxy object". The actual query to load the children executes only if and when you explicitly call the getter method (e.g., parent.getChildren()).
• When to Use: The recommended default setting for collections (@OneToMany, @ManyToMany).
• Risk: Throws a LazyInitializationException if you attempt to call the child getter method after the database transaction/EntityManager session has already closed.
