Here is a practical, production-ready implementation of combining multiple asynchronous API calls using CompletableFuture.

The Scenario

Imagine building an API gateway aggregator for an e-commerce dashboard. To compile a single UserDashboard object, you must fetch data from three separate microservices:
1. User Profile Service (Fast)
2. Order History Service (Slow)
3. Recommendation Service (Independent of orders, but requires the user profile)
We will execute these tasks concurrently and combine their results efficiently without blocking the main application thread.

1. Consolidated Code Implementation

java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class AsyncDashboardAggregator {

    // Define a custom thread pool dedicated to external I/O tasks 
    // to avoid starving the default ForkJoinPool.commonPool().
    private final ExecutorService ioExecutor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    // Mock Domain Models
    public record UserProfile(String userId, String name, String tier) {}
    public record Order(String orderId, double amount) {}
    public record Recommendation(String itemId, String name) {}
    public record UserDashboard(UserProfile profile, List<Order> orders, List<Recommendation> recommendations) {}

    public UserDashboard getUserDashboard(String userId) {
        System.out.println("Starting dashboard aggregation on thread: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());

        // 1. Fetch User Profile Asynchronously
        CompletableFuture<UserProfile> profileFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> fetchUserProfile(userId), ioExecutor);

        // 2. Fetch Order History Asynchronously (Runs in parallel with profile)
        CompletableFuture<List<Order>> ordersFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> fetchOrderHistory(userId), ioExecutor)
                .exceptionally(ex -> {
                    System.err.println("Failed to fetch orders, returning empty fallback list: " + ex.getMessage());
                    return List.of(); // Graceful fallback
                });

        // 3. Fetch Recommendations DEPENDING on the User Profile (thenCompose)
        // thenCompose flattens the nested CompletableFuture structure (like flatMap)
        CompletableFuture<List<Recommendation>> recommendationsFuture = profileFuture.thenComposeAsync(
                profile -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchRecommendations(profile), ioExecutor), 
                ioExecutor
        );

        // 4. Combine all independent futures when they ALL finish
        UserDashboard combinedDashboard = CompletableFuture.allOf(profileFuture, ordersFuture, recommendationsFuture)
                .thenApply(v -> {
                    // All futures are guaranteed to be complete at this block
                    UserProfile profile = profileFuture.join();
                    List<Order> orders = ordersFuture.join();
                    List<Recommendation> recommendations = recommendationsFuture.join();
                    
                    return new UserDashboard(profile, orders, recommendations);
                })
                .orTimeout(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS) // Prevent hung threads by setting a global SLA timeout
                .join(); // Block safely here to return the final aggregated object to the client

        return combinedDashboard;
    }

    // Mock API call implementations simulate network delays
    private UserProfile fetchUserProfile(String userId) {
        delay(300); // 300ms delay
        return new UserProfile(userId, "John Doe", "PREMIUM");
    }

    private List<Order> fetchOrderHistory(String userId) {
        delay(800); // 800ms delay
        return List.of(new Order("TXN-101", 120.50), new Order("TXN-102", 45.00));
    }

    private List<Recommendation> fetchRecommendations(UserProfile profile) {
        delay(400); // 400ms delay
        if ("PREMIUM".equals(profile.tier())) {
            return List.of(new Recommendation("REC-01", "Luxury Watch"), new Recommendation("REC-02", "Noise Cancelling Headphones"));
        }
        return List.of(new Recommendation("REC-03", "Standard Sneakers"));
    }

    private void delay(long milliseconds) {
        try { Thread.sleep(milliseconds); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AsyncDashboardAggregator aggregator = new AsyncDashboardAggregator();
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        
        UserDashboard dashboard = aggregator.getUserDashboard("user_99");
        
        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        System.out.println("\n--- Aggregation Complete ---");
        System.out.println("Profile: " + dashboard.profile().name());
        System.out.println("Orders Count: " + dashboard.orders().size());
        System.out.println("Recommendations: " + dashboard.recommendations().size());
        System.out.println("Total Execution Time: " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
        
        aggregator.ioExecutor.shutdown();
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Key Architecture Mechanics Explained

• CompletableFuture.allOf(...): This acts as an asynchronous barrier. It orchestrates execution, moving to the calculation phase only when every supplied future is done. This prevents individual thread synchronization cleanups.
• thenComposeAsync: We used this for the Recommendation service because it depends on the result of the profile service. Instead of creating a messy CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<List<Recommendation>>>, thenComposeAsync flattens the pipeline cleanly.
• .exceptionally(): Implements resilience. If the Order service goes down and throws an exception, the pipeline doesn't crash; instead, it catches the error and falls back gracefully to an empty list List.of().
• Custom ExecutorService: By default, CompletableFuture runs tasks inside the global ForkJoinPool.commonPool(). If your microservice API threads block on network calls, you can saturate this pool and lock up internal framework processing. Passing a dedicated ioExecutor completely isolates web calling workloads.
• .orTimeout(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS) (Java 9+): Protects your microservice from stalling if an upstream service undergoes a cascading failure or suffers infinite latency. It throws a TimeoutException if things take too long.

