Here is a clean, modern example of a Kafka Producer and Kafka Consumer written in Java.
This example uses the official Apache Kafka client library. To run this code, make sure you have the Kafka client dependency added to your project (e.g., org.apache.kafka:kafka-clients in Maven or Gradle).

1. The Kafka Producer

The producer connects to the Kafka brokers and sends messages (records) to a specific topic.
java
package com.example.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.RecordMetadata;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;

import java.util.Properties;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;

public class SampleProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String bootstrapServers = "localhost:9092";
        String topic = "sample-topic";

        // 1. Define Producer Configuration Properties
        Properties properties = new Properties();
        properties.setProperty(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, bootstrapServers);
        // Kafka transfers raw bytes; serializers tell it how to convert Java objects to bytes
        properties.setProperty(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        properties.setProperty(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());

        // 2. Create the Producer instance
        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(properties)) {
            
            System.out.println("--- Starting Kafka Producer ---");

            // 3. Send a few sample messages
            for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                String key = "id_" + i;
                String value = "Hello Kafka World Number " + i;

                // Create the record (Topic, Key, Value)
                ProducerRecord<String, String> record = new ProducerRecord<>(topic, key, value);

                // Send data asynchronously and track the delivery status using a callback
                producer.send(record, (RecordMetadata metadata, Exception exception) -> {
                    if (exception == null) {
                        System.out.printf("Sent successfully! Topic: %s | Partition: %d | Offset: %d | Key: %s%n",
                                metadata.topic(), metadata.partition(), metadata.offset(), key);
                    } else {
                        System.err.println("Error while producing message: " + exception.getMessage());
                    }
                });
            }

            // 4. Flush remaining buffered messages to Kafka before closing
            producer.flush();
            System.out.println("--- All messages dispatched ---");
        } 
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. The Kafka Consumer

The consumer runs continuously, pulling batches of messages from the topic and tracking its read progress using a consumer group identifier.
java
package com.example.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class SampleConsumer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String bootstrapServers = "localhost:9092";
        String topic = "sample-topic";
        String groupId = "my-java-consumer-group";

        // 1. Define Consumer Configuration Properties
        Properties properties = new Properties();
        properties.setProperty(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, bootstrapServers);
        // Deserializers turn the broker's raw bytes back into Java Strings
        properties.setProperty(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        properties.setProperty(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        properties.setProperty(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, groupId);
        // "earliest" reads from the start of the topic if no previous offsets exist
        properties.setProperty(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, "earliest");

        // 2. Create the Consumer instance
        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(properties)) {
            
            // 3. Subscribe to our target topic
            consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList(topic));
            System.out.println("--- Kafka Consumer Started. Listening for messages... ---");

            // 4. Poll Loop: Continuously request data from the Kafka cluster
            while (true) {
                // Poll Kafka for new data every 100 milliseconds
                ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));

                for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
                    System.out.printf("Received Message -> Key: %s | Value: %s | Partition: %d | Offset: %d%n",
                            record.key(), record.value(), record.partition(), record.offset());
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Consumer encountered an error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Quick Requirements Checklist to Run This:

1. Running Broker: Make sure a local Kafka broker is running at localhost:9092 (via Docker or local binary install).
2. Topic Creation: You can create the topic manually beforehand using Kafka's CLI utilities, or rely on Kafka's auto-topic-creation setting (auto.create.topics.enable=true) on the broker.
3. Execution Order: Start the SampleConsumer first so it is listening, and then execute the SampleProducer to see the messages appear instantly in the consumer console window.

